        "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ x(x+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=-6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/7d9fd824b1e4d63e417e002c07a01adb4c14f73fc81be66c31a5c9aa30ab7ffd.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 0.$",
